<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Birzali</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad ibn Abd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> al-Birzali was leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-birzal">Banu Birzal</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> clan brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> as soldiers by al-Mansur. During the civil war that destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> he took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmona">Carmona</a> around 1013 and made it the base of an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a>. He and his successors contested the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Guadalquivir</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbadid-dynasty">Abbadids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, who finally annexed Carmona in 1067.</p></div>
